The neuron goes negative on the word that opens a subordinate or relative clause (the
'that', 'which', 'as', 'if', 'when' of a sentence), and is near zero everywhere else.
It is strongest in Russian: что (that/what), чтобы (in order that), когда (when),
если (if), чем (than), который/которые (which), где (where), как (as/how). It does the
same job in other languages: Czech/Slovak který and že, Hungarian hogy, German dass,
daß, wenn, welche, Polish że and aby, Arabic التي, Chinese 如果.
English is weak: 'which' fires only after a preposition ('in which', 'by which'), and
'whom', 'otherwise', 'whereas', 'For example' fire a little. English 'that', 'if', 'when',
